Basic Networking Commands in CMD (Command Prompt):

1. ipconfig
Shows your current IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway.

2. ipconfig /all
Displays detailed network configuration, including MAC address and DNS servers.

3. ipconfig /release
Releases the current IP address (if using DHCP).

4. ipconfig /renew
Requests a new IP address from the DHCP server.

5. ping [IP or domain]
Tests the connectivity to a remote host (e.g., ping google.com).

6. tracert [domain]
Traces the path that a packet takes to reach a destination.

7. nslookup [domain]
Retrieves the IP address associated with a domain name using DNS.

8. netstat
Shows active network connections and open ports.

9. netstat -an
Displays connections and listening ports with IP addresses and port numbers.

10. arp -a
Displays the ARP table (maps IP addresses to MAC addresses).

11. getmac
Shows the MAC address of your network adapter.

12. hostname
Displays the name of your computer on the network.

13. route print
Shows the routing table of your device.

14. netsh
A powerful tool to configure network settings via the command line.

15. netsh wlan show profiles
Lists all saved Wi-Fi profiles on your computer.

16. netsh wlan show profile name="NetworkName" key=clear
Reveals the saved password for a specific Wi-Fi profile.

17. telnet [IP] [port]
Tests connection to a specific port (Telnet client must be enabled).

18. pathping [domain or IP]
Combines ping and tracert for a detailed network path analysis.

19. tasklist
Displays a list of all running processes.

20. taskkill /pid [PID] /f
Forcefully terminates a process by its Process ID.
